# EphCore.TimeSeries — Grid Computing Tour

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:eph_core, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
    {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
  ],
  config: [
    eph_core: [
      # Planetary DE440s only. Avoid config_path: :eph_core here — that loads
      # config.exs asteroid kernels before SPK.Server starts.
      kernel_base_dir: Path.expand("../../priv", __DIR__),
      spk_kernels: ["de440s.bsp"]
    ]
  ]
)

Logger.configure(level: :warning)
```

## Overview

Batch ephemeris for charts and event searches. `TimeSeries.compute/3` evaluates a
grid of timestamps in one pass — ecliptic longitudes, declinations, and motion
rates — while `add_horizon_geometry/2` layers on alt/az, LST, and the
Ascendant/MC axes. Useful for charts, motion curves, and horizon overlays.

**How to open:** Install [Livebook](https://livebook.dev/), open this `.livemd`,
evaluate the setup cell. Kernels must already be under `eph_core/priv/ephemeris/`
(run `mix eph.download_kernels` in the repo first; the setup cell will not start without baseline files).

## Helpers

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

# Kino.Input.utc_datetime/2 reads as NaiveDateTime (UTC) or DateTime depending on version.
read_utc = fn input ->
  case Kino.Input.read(input) do
    %DateTime{} = dt ->
      DateTime.truncate(dt, :second)

    %NaiveDateTime{} = ndt ->
      ndt |> DateTime.from_naive!("Etc/UTC") |> DateTime.truncate(:second)
  end
end

:ok
```

## Ready check

```elixir
priv = EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.base_dir()
spk = Path.join([priv, "ephemeris", "spk", "de440s.bsp"])

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**EphCore** `#{Application.spec(:eph_core, :vsn)}` · priv `#{priv}`

SPK `de440s.bsp`: #{if File.exists?(spk), do: "✅ present", else: "❌ missing — run `mix eph.download_kernels`"}
""")
```

## Observer & time range

Minneapolis defaults — edit and re-evaluate. We build 96 timestamps spanning
one UTC day at 15-minute intervals, light enough to keep Livebook snappy.

```elixir
lat_in = Kino.Input.number("Latitude (°)", default: 44.9778)
lon_in = Kino.Input.number("Longitude (°)", default: -93.2650)
height_in = Kino.Input.number("Height (m)", default: 250)
start_in = Kino.Input.utc_datetime("Start UTC", default: ~N[2026-07-21 00:00:00])

Kino.Layout.grid([lat_in, lon_in, height_in, start_in], columns: 2)
```

```elixir
observer = %{
  lat: Kino.Input.read(lat_in),
  lon: Kino.Input.read(lon_in),
  height: Kino.Input.read(height_in)
}

start_dt = read_utc.(start_in)

timestamps =
  for i <- 0..95 do
    DateTime.add(start_dt, i * 15 * 60, :second)
  end

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**#{length(timestamps)} samples** from `#{DateTime.to_iso8601(List.first(timestamps))}`
to `#{DateTime.to_iso8601(List.last(timestamps))}` (15-min cadence)

Observer: `#{observer.lat}°, #{observer.lon}°` at #{observer.height} m
""")
```

## Compute the grid

`compute/3` returns ecliptic longitudes (true-of-date), equatorial declinations
(J2000 ICRF), and motion rates (°/day via central difference) for every body at
every timestamp. Pass `:observer` for topocentric; omit it for geocentric.

```elixir
bodies = [:sun, :moon, :mars, :jupiter, :saturn]

{us, {:ok, grid}} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    EphCore.TimeSeries.compute(timestamps, bodies, observer: observer)
  end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
Grid: **#{length(bodies)} bodies × #{length(timestamps)} timestamps** computed in
**#{Float.round(us / 1000, 1)} ms**

Keys: `#{inspect(Map.keys(grid) |> Enum.sort())}`
""")
```

### Positions snapshot

Ecliptic longitudes at the first, middle, and last timestamps.

```elixir
sample_indices = [0, div(length(timestamps) - 1, 2), length(timestamps) - 1]

pos_rows =
  for i <- sample_indices, body <- bodies do
    %{
      utc: DateTime.to_iso8601(Enum.at(grid.timestamps, i)),
      body: body,
      ecl_lon_deg: Float.round(Enum.at(grid.positions[body], i), 4),
      dec_deg: Float.round(Enum.at(grid.declinations[body], i), 4),
      rate_deg_day: Float.round(Enum.at(grid.motion[body], i), 4)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(pos_rows, name: "Position samples (start / mid / end)")
```

### Motion direction

Positive rates mean direct motion; negative means retrograde. Quick scan across
all bodies at the midpoint.

```elixir
mid = div(length(timestamps) - 1, 2)

motion_rows =
  for body <- bodies do
    rate = Enum.at(grid.motion[body], mid)

    %{
      body: body,
      rate_deg_day: Float.round(rate, 4),
      direction: if(rate >= 0, do: "direct", else: "retrograde")
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(motion_rows, name: "Motion at midpoint")
```

## Add horizon geometry

`add_horizon_geometry/2` augments the grid with per-timestamp altitude, azimuth,
local sidereal time, obliquity, and the Ascendant/MC ecliptic longitudes.

```elixir
{hz_us, grid_hz} =
  :timer.tc(fn -> EphCore.TimeSeries.add_horizon_geometry(grid, observer) end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
Horizon geometry added in **#{Float.round(hz_us / 1000, 1)} ms**.

New keys: `:axes`, `:altitudes`, `:azimuths`, `:lst`, `:obliquity_deg`, `:observer_lat`
""")
```

```elixir
horizon_rows =
  for i <- sample_indices, body <- bodies do
    %{
      utc: DateTime.to_iso8601(Enum.at(grid_hz.timestamps, i)),
      body: body,
      alt_deg: Float.round(Enum.at(grid_hz.altitudes[body], i), 2),
      az_deg: Float.round(Enum.at(grid_hz.azimuths[body], i), 2),
      lst_deg: Float.round(Enum.at(grid_hz.lst, i), 2),
      asc_deg: Float.round(Enum.at(grid_hz.axes.asc, i), 2),
      mc_deg: Float.round(Enum.at(grid_hz.axes.mc, i), 2)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(horizon_rows, name: "Horizon geometry samples")
```

## Charts — ecliptic longitude vs time

Flatten the grid into chart-friendly rows, then plot ecliptic longitude curves
for each body over the day.

```elixir
lon_rows =
  for i <- 0..(length(timestamps) - 1), body <- bodies do
    %{
      hour: i * 0.25,
      body: Atom.to_string(body),
      ecl_lon_deg: Enum.at(grid.positions[body], i)
    }
  end

Vl.new(width: 560, height: 320, title: "Ecliptic longitude over 24 h")
|> Vl.data_from_values(lon_rows)
|> Vl.mark(:line, tooltip: true)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "hour", type: :quantitative, title: "Hours from start")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "ecl_lon_deg", type: :quantitative, title: "Ecliptic longitude (°)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "body", type: :nominal)
```

### Altitude curve

Same data, switched to altitude — watch bodies rise and set across the day.

```elixir
alt_rows =
  for i <- 0..(length(timestamps) - 1), body <- bodies do
    %{
      hour: i * 0.25,
      body: Atom.to_string(body),
      alt_deg: Enum.at(grid_hz.altitudes[body], i)
    }
  end

Vl.new(width: 560, height: 320, title: "Altitude over 24 h")
|> Vl.data_from_values(alt_rows)
|> Vl.mark(:line, tooltip: true)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "hour", type: :quantitative, title: "Hours from start")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "alt_deg", type: :quantitative, title: "Altitude (°)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "body", type: :nominal)
|> Vl.encode(:stroke_dash,
  condition: %{test: "datum.alt_deg < 0", value: [4, 4]},
  value: []
)
```

## TimeSeries vs SnapshotPipeline

`TimeSeries` is the **grid path** — it computes positions across hundreds of
timestamps in a single sweep with no light-time iteration or aberration
correction. That makes it fast for charts, motion curves, and event scanning
where a few arcseconds of systematic offset are acceptable.

`SnapshotPipeline` is the **snapshot path** — it evaluates a single instant with
full corrections (light-time, aberration, precession/nutation). It is what a
station or extremum *refinement* step calls via `position_fn` once a grid
candidate is identified. The two paths share the same topocentric frame (GMST rotation +
true-of-date ecliptic), so grid minima and refined exacts agree to within
scanning tolerance.

```elixir
ms_per_sample = Float.round(us / 1000 / length(timestamps), 2)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
This run: TimeSeries averaged **#{ms_per_sample} ms/sample**
(#{length(timestamps)} samples, #{Float.round(us / 1000, 1)} ms wall).
""")
```

|                       | TimeSeries (grid)                      | SnapshotPipeline (snapshot)        |
| --------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| **Use case**          | Charts, event searches, motion curves  | Precise single-instant observation |
| **Light-time**        | No                                     | Yes                                |
| **Aberration**        | No                                     | Yes                                |
| **Topocentric frame** | GMST, true-of-date ecliptic            | Same (by design)                   |
| **Typical call**      | `compute/3` + `add_horizon_geometry/2` | `observe/4`                        |
| **Speed**             | ~0.1–1 ms/sample (grid)              | ~2–5 ms/sample                   |

## Key ideas

* **Grid-first architecture** — scan cheaply, refine precisely. TimeSeries
  builds the grid; SnapshotPipeline refines the minima. Same frame, different
  fidelity.
* **Central-difference motion** — rates reuse already-computed longitudes;
  no extra SPK evaluations.
* **Horizon geometry is optional** — `add_horizon_geometry/2` layers on
  alt/az, ASC/MC, and LST only when the caller needs overlay substrates.
* **Scaling lever** — want finer resolution? Shrink the interval from 15 min
  to 5 min. Want a wider sweep? Extend timestamps to a week. The grid handles
  both.
* **Frame agreement** — topocentric longitudes from the grid match refinement
  within scanning tolerance because both paths use the same GMST + true-of-date
  ecliptic rotation.
